I've grasped all the wiring information. Next, I'll create the KillFeed component. Reflecting toast-stack's patterns:
- `#kill-feed` div, top center, flex column, pointerEvents none, aria-live polite, monospace font.
- Entry text: `killer ▸ weapon ▸ victim`
- pvp → white #ffffff, otherwise gray #8b97ab
- Per-entry setTimeout(KILL_FEED_TTL_MS) → removeKillFeedEntry(id); schedule only once per entry ID; clear everything on unmount.
- CSS keyframes that fade the last 2 seconds (optional). I'll add a fade-out animation of 10 seconds where opacity 1 lasts for 8 seconds and fades over the final 2 seconds.

The component subscribes to killFeedSubscribe, maintains a set of items, and uses a ref Map for the timers keyed by entry ID. For each new entry ID not yet in timers, schedule removal. On unmount, clear all timers.

Implementation:

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import {
  KILL_FEED_TTL_MS,
  killFeedEntries,
  killFeedSubscribe,
  removeKillFeedEntry,
  type KillFeedEntry,
} from '@client/state/kill-feed';
```

Check the import style: toast-stack imports `@client/net/presence`. main.tsx also uses `@client/...`. I'll verify the tsconfig aliases. `@client/` and `@shared/` are being used. OK.

```tsx
/**
 * TASK-47: persistent kill feed (top center, most recent 5, 10-second fade).
 * Fed only from kill events...
 */
export function KillFeed() {
  const [items, setItems] = React.useState<KillFeedEntry[]>(() => killFeedEntries());
  const timers = React.useRef(new Map<number, ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>>());

  React.useEffect(
    () =>
      killFeedSubscribe((entries) => {
        setItems(entries);
        // Schedule TTL removal once per entry ID.
        for (const e of entries) {
          if (!timers.current.has(e.id)) {
            timers.current.set(
              e.id,
              setTimeout(() => removeKillFeedEntry(e.id), KILL_FEED_TTL_MS),
            );
          }
        }
        for (const [id, timer] of timers.current) {
          if (!entries.some((e) => e.id === id)) {
            clearTimeout(timer);
            timers.current.delete(id);
          }
        }
      }),
    [],
  );

  React.useEffect(
    () => () => {
      for (const timer of timers.current.values()) clearTimeout(timer);
      timers.current.clear();
    },
    [],
  );

  return (
    <div id="kill-feed" style={styles.feed} aria-live="polite">
      <style>{keyframes}</style>
      {items.map((e) => (
        <div key={e.id} style={{ ...styles.entry, color: e.pvp ? '#ffffff' : '#8b97ab' }}>
          {`${e.killer} ▸ ${e.weapon} ▸ ${e.victim}`}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Wait — there's a subtle point about scheduling timers inside the subscribe callback: the initial call `fn(current)` fires synchronously during the effect, so that's fine.

However, one issue: the store's `pushKillEvent` does TTL pruning at push time; the per-entry timer removes at exactly TTL_MS. No problem.

Keyframes: fade the last 2 seconds:

```
@keyframes kill-feed-life {
  0%   { opacity: 0; }
  4%   { opacity: 1; }
  80%  { opacity: 1; }
  100% { opacity: 0; }
}
```

10-second duration, opacity 1 until 80% (8 seconds), then fade over the last 2 seconds.

